## 4. Auth

Log in once per family in the setup project and save `storageState` (`AUTH_FILE` for the main family, `ALT_AUTH_FILE` for the second). A two-family test opens a second context with `browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE })`. Credentials come from `process.env`. Never log in per test.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — setup project (auth.setup.ts)
await loginPage.login(process.env.USER_EMAIL!, process.env.USER_PASSWORD!);
await page.context().storageState({ path: AUTH_FILE });

// two-family test
const altContext = await browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE });
const altPage = await altContext.newPage();

// ❌ Bad
test('creates a booking', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('student@example.com'); // hardcoded + per-test login
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('Password123!');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
});
```

**Why:** Shared `storageState` keeps suites fast and avoids credential sprawl; env-sourced secrets stay out of the repo.

