## 9. Network mocking

Use `page.route` / `route.fulfill` for 500, 503, timeout, empty list, and malformed payload. Never mock the endpoint under test.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — exercise error UI while the happy-path API stays real elsewhere
await page.route('**/api/bookings', async (route) => {
  await route.fulfill({ status: 503, body: JSON.stringify({ error: 'unavailable' }) });
});
await page.goto('/bookings');
await expect(page.getByRole('alert')).toContainText('unavailable');

// ❌ Bad — mocking the same endpoint the test claims to verify
test('creates a booking via API @api', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.route('**/api/bookings', (route) =>
    route.fulfill({ status: 201, body: JSON.stringify({ id: 'fake' }) }),
  );
});
```

**Why:** Mocks belong to negative and edge paths; mocking the subject under test turns the suite into a self-fulfilling stub.

